Z||000000||bHooper||06-12-2001||09:14 AM||||i need to adjust the belt on my SE, it is constantly squeeling.  what is a good, easy method to adjusting this belt on the automatic cars?<P>i can do it on my 5 spd GT, but there is much more in the way on this car!<P>Thanks!<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||06-12-2001||11:58 AM||||Buy the $7 belt tightener from J.C.Whitney (it's a fancy turnbuckle)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||06-12-2001||04:45 PM||||I agree with Phil.  I've used mine on many projects and it is a great help.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||04:51 PM||||on most cars I can find a place to wedge my 1/2" craftman breaker bar in between the alt and the engine block, and use it as a lever to tension the belt.<P>   havent had to do it to a fiero yet so I dont know if that will work.<P>  but you defiantely need to get some leverage on the alt body to tension the belt.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PBJ||06-12-2001||08:50 PM||||I would (I did) use a pry bar between the engine and the alt and use the $7.00 for Taco Bell after the job was done rather than on the tool.  The tool is handy, but...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||06-12-2001||09:35 PM||||i think i'm gonna live with the slipping belt for a week, until i'm able to work on the manifold (the job that i was supposed to do on friday, but got distracted from) and have the cradle loose.<P>that way i can properly adjust it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>i did jack the car up this afternoon, and looked at that altenator... man is that a monstor!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
